- How would you modify for 32-bit inputs?
- Compare this to using built-ins like __builtin_ctzll in terms of portability and code size.

Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about 128-bit values and apply bit-twiddling without branching. This requires cross-half logic and edge-case handling.

Key Concepts
- 128-bit decomposition into hi and lo
- a 64-bit value is a power of two if v & (v-1) == 0 and v != 0
- exactly one half nonzero implies overall power-of-two only if that half is itself a power of two

Code Example

```c
uint8_t isPowerOfTwo128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  // Exactly one half must be nonzero and that half must be a power of two
  int hiPow = (hi & (hi - 1)) == 0 && hi != 0;
  int loPow = (lo & (lo - 1)) == 0 && lo != 0;
  return ((hi == 0) ^ (lo == 0)) ? (hi ? hiPow : loPow) : 0;
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for a specific CPU architecture (e.g., using intrinsics)?
- How would you extend this to 256-bit values?

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about data-parallel bit tricks and boundary-safe shifts without branches.

## Key Concepts

- Byte-wise (SWAR) operations across 128-bit lanes
- Constructing per-byte masks to constrain operations to byte boundaries
- Handling n in 0..7 without loops or conditionals

## Code Example

```cpp
// outline of approach (conceptual; leverages 128-bit value split into bytes)
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 perByteRotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
    uint128 M = { 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL };
    uint128 left  = { .hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (8 - n)), .lo = x.lo << n };
    uint128 right = { .hi = (x.hi >> (8 - n)), .lo = (x.lo >> (8 - n)) };
    return { .hi = (left.hi & M.hi) | (right.hi & ~M.hi),
             .lo = (left.lo & M.lo) | (right.lo & ~M.lo) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 32-bit lanes or to 256-bit types?
- What are the performance implications on modern compilers and ISAs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate multi-word integers with precise bit logic, no loops, and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit two-word shifts without loops
- x & ~(x<<1) to locate run-start bits
- 64-bit popcount for final tally

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t countOneRuns128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t lo_shift = lo << 1;
  uint64_t hi_shift = (hi << 1) | (lo >> 63);
  uint64_t mask_lo = lo & ~lo_shift;
  uint64_t mask_hi = hi & ~hi_shift;
  return __builtin_popcountll(mask_lo) + __builtin_popcountll(mask_hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for counting 0-runs?
- What's the time complexity compared to a loop-based solution?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply known bit-hack patterns to solve a common problem without loops or libs. Shows understanding of data-parallel aggregation and portability across compilers.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) counting
- Bit masks and shifts to sum bits in chunks
- Portability across 32-bit integers

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
uint32_t countBits32(uint32_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
  return x;
}
```

